      <floats-group id="article-27295.s18">
        <fig id="article-27295.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>True Koebner Phenomenon in a Patient With Chronic Plaque Psoriasis. The kissing lesions of psoriasis are observed along the natal cleft stemming from trivial friction-induced trauma. Contributed by S Sonthalia, MD, DNB, MNAMS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Fig__1" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-27295.s19">
        <fig id="article-27295.image.f3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Pathogenesis of Psoriasis. Triggers such as trauma, infection, drugs, or autoantigens activate neutrophils, keratinocytes, and dendritic cells, initiating T-cell&#x02013;driven inflammation. Activated TH17, TH22, TH1, and Tc17 cells release cytokines (IL-17, IL-22, and IFN-&#x003b3;) that drive keratinocyte hyperproliferation, angiogenesis, and sustained immune infiltration. This pathogenic cascade underscores the rationale for targeted therapies, including IL-17, IL-23, and TNF inhibitors. IL, interleukin; TNF-&#x003b1;, tumor necrosis factor-alpha; IFN, interferon; KC, keratinocyte; LC, Langerhans cell; DC, dendritic cell; AMPs, antimicrobial peptides; TLR, Toll-like receptor; NETs, neutrophil extracellular traps; MMP9, matrix metalloproteinase-9; &#x003b3;&#x003b4;T cell, gamma delta T cell; Th, T helper; PASI, Psoriasis Area and Severity Index. Sieminska&#x000a0;I, Pieniawska M, Grzywa TM. The immunology of psoriasis&#x02014;current concepts in pathogenesis. <italic toggle="yes">Clin Rev Allergy Immunol.</italic> 2024&#x0037e;66(2):164-191. doi: 10.1007/s12016-024-08991-7.&#x000a0;</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Psoriasis__pathogenesis__1" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-27295.s20">
        <fig id="article-27295.image.f4" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Psoriasis Dermpath. The image shows fully developed plaques with parakeratosis, loss of the granular layer, elongated and clubbed rete ridges, suprapapillary epidermal thinning, dilated papillary dermal vessels, and mixed inflammatory infiltrates with Munro microabscesses. Contributed by N Sathe, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Psoriasis" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-27295.s21">
        <fig id="article-27295.image.f5" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Psoriasis: Annular Erythematous Plaques on the Arm. Actively spreading psoriasis lesions display a brightly erythematous advancing edge, whereas central involution produces annular-shaped plaques on the skin. Contributed by S Mehta, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="ps" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-27295.s22">
        <fig id="article-27295.image.f6" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Nails in Psoriasis. Psoriatic nail changes include pitting, onycholysis, subungual hyperkeratosis, and oil-drop (salmon patch) discoloration. Nail involvement is often associated with psoriatic arthritis. Contributed by N Waqas, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="c4b1605d-5cbb-4b7a-a0d3-b15194d0d98f" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
